      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We have two cases to consider: when $n + 6 \\ge 0$ and $n + 6 < 0$. \n\n(1) When $n + 6 \\ge 0$, we have $n + 6 = 2 - n$.\nSimplifying, we get $2n = -4$, so $n = -2$.\nChecking, when $n = -2$, we have $|-2 + 6| = |-4| = 4$, which matches the right side of the equation.\n\n(2) When $n + 6 < 0$, we have $-(n + 6) = 2 - n$.\nSimplifying, we get $-n - 6 = 2 - n$, which is true for all $n$.\nHowever, we checked that $n = -2$ satisfies the original equation, so this case is not valid in this context.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $n = \\boxed{-2}$.The answer is: -2",
